\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The masses at interpolated Wilson zeros}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Take the free Wilson symbol of \ref{fdip-0142} on periods #{N_j\ge 3}, with the affine interpolants on the \vocabk{simplicial momentum grid}{fdip-013X}. Its centered symbol vanishes precisely where each component is #{0} or #{\pi}. At the zero with #{\pi} on a subset #{J} of axes the local degree is #{(-1)^{|J|}}. The interpolated unshifted dimensionless Wilson mass there is #{\sum_{j\in J}b_j}, with #{b_j=2} for even #{N_j} and #{b_j=1+\cos(\pi /N_j)} for odd #{N_j}.}

\proof{\p{On a coordinate cube, linearly interpolate the one-variable samples of #{\sin p_j} and #{1-\cos p_j} along that axis. These functions, and their sums over axes, are affine on the cube and agree at all its vertices with the separable samples. Their restrictions to each simplex are therefore its prescribed affine interpolants. The sine changes sign at zero with positive slope on its two neighboring intervals. On an even axis the same calculation at #{\pi} has negative slope on both neighboring intervals. On an odd axis the two samples next to #{\pi} are opposite sines at equidistant angles, so their segment crosses zero exactly at #{\pi} with negative slope. Other sample intervals have no additional zero. On a small product neighborhood of any resulting zero these one-variable interpolants are strictly monotone, giving local degree equal to their orientation product for the [local degree](fdip-013Y). The Wilson term is zero on a zero component and equals #{2} at an even sampled #{\pi}. The two odd neighbors have the same mass #{1+\cos(\pi /N_j)}, which their whole connecting segment retains.}}
